<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Bangli</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 CE – 1909 CE (approx.) · capital Bangli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Landlocked kingdom of the Balinese highlands, home of the Kehen temple and the lands around Lake Batur.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bangli was the only Balinese kingdom without a coastline. Its ruling house was a branch of the noble lineages tied to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-klungkung">Kingdom of Klungkung</a>, and it emerged as a separate kingdom by about 1800. Its documented history is thin compared with the coastal courts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> terraces of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintamani">Kintamani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-batur">Lake Batur</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali-aga">Bali Aga</a> villages kept older customs. Its state temple, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pura-kehen">Pura Kehen</a>, is one of the oldest on the island. Bangli was often at war with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gianyar">Gianyar</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-klungkung">Klungkung</a>, and allied itself with whichever court served its interests.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1909, after the conquests of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-badung">Badung</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tabanan">Tabanan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-klungkung">Klungkung</a>, Bangli accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> protection, with its ruler kept on as regent within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. It is now a regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> province.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1800 CE – 1909 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.52%;width:2.17%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 CE – 1909 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bangli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Balinese Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Balinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bangli</dd></dl></aside></div>

